":"2026-10-30","vl":"Guðni Jóhannesson"},{"qid":"Q189","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q310396","value_label":"Kristján Eldjárn","from_t":1968.5819672131147,"to_t":1980.5792349726776,"title":"President of Iceland","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Kristján Eldjárn"},{"qid":"Q189","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q57284","value_label":"Ólafur Ragnar Grímsson","from_t":1996.5819672131147,"to_t":2016.5792349726776,"title":"President of Iceland","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Ólafur Ragnar Grímsson"},{"qid":"Q189","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q93335","value_label":"Flag of Iceland.svg","from_t":1944,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Iceland","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Iceland"}],"people":{"Q156617":{"label":"Christian X of Denmark","description":"King of Denmark (1912–1947) and Iceland (1918–1944)","enwiki":"Christian X","summary":"Christian X (Danish: Christian Carl Frederik Albert Alexander Vilhelm; 26 September 1870 – 20 April 1947) was King of Denmark from 1912 until his death in 1947, and the only King of Iceland as Kristján X, holding the title as a result of the personal union between Denmark and independent Iceland between 1918 and 1944.\nHe was a member of the House of Glücksburg, a branch of the House of Oldenburg, and the first monarch since King Frederik VII born into the Danish royal family; both his father and his grandfather were born as princes of a ducal family from Schleswig. Among his siblings was King Haakon VII of Norway. His son became King Frederik IX of Denmark.","image":"Christian X of Denmark circa 1915.jpg","birth":1870.7342465753425,"death":1947.2986301369863},"Q24494577":{"label":"Halla Tómasdóttir","description":"President of Iceland","enwiki":"Halla Tómasdóttir","summary":"Halla Tómasdóttir (Icelandic pronunciation: [ˈhatla ˈtʰouːmasˌtouhtɪr̥] ; born 11 October 1968) is an Icelandic businesswoman and politician who has served as president of Iceland since 2024. She is Iceland's second female president, after Vigdís Finnbogadóttir. Before becoming president, Halla was a public speaker and the chief executive of The B Team, a global nonprofit group.","image":"Halla Tómasdóttir at Arctic Circle 2024 cropped.jpg","birth":1968.775956284153,"death":null},"Q108856371":{"label":"Kristrún Mjöll Frostadóttir","description":"Icelandic politician","enwiki":"Kristrún Frostadóttir","summary":"Kristrún Mjöll Frostadóttir (Icelandic: [ˈkʰrɪstrun ˈmjœtl̥ ˈfrɔstaˌtouhtɪr̥]; born 12 May 1988) is an Icelandic politician and economist who has served as the prime minister of Iceland since 2024, and leader of the Social Democratic Alliance since 2022. First elected to the Althing in the 2021 parliamentary election, she became Iceland's youngest-ever prime minister at the age of 36. As of March 2026, she is the world's second-youngest serving head of government behind Balen Shah.","image":"NYCW 2026 - Kristrún Frostadóttir 09 (cropped).jpg","birth":1988.360655737705,"death":null},"Q189797":{"label":"Vigdís Finnbogadóttir","description":"Icelandic politician, 4th President of Iceland","enwiki":"Vigdís Finnbogadóttir","summary":"Vigdís Finnbogadóttir (Icelandic: [ˈvɪɣtis ˈfɪnpɔɣaˌtouʰtɪr̥] ; born 15 April 1930) is an Icelandic stateswoman and theatre director who served as the president of Iceland, from 1980 to 1996, the first woman to hold the position and the first in the world to be democratically elected president of a country. Having served for 16 years, she was also the longest-serving elected female head of state in history. Vigdís is a UNESCO Goodwill Ambassador and a member of the Club of Madrid.","image":"Vigdis Finnbogadottir (1985).jpg","birth":1930.2849315068493,"death":null},"Q207206":{"label":"Sveinn Björnsson","description":"Icelandic politician, 1st President of Iceland (1881-1952)","enwiki":"Sveinn Björnsson","summary":"Sveinn Björnsson (Icelandic: [ˈsveitn̥ ˈpjœsːɔn]; 27 February 1881 – 25 January 1952) was the first president of Iceland, serving from 1944 until his death in 1952.","image":"Sveinn Björnsson.jpg","birth":1881.1561643835616,"death":1952.0655737704917},"Q217848":{"label":"Ásgeir Ásgeirsson","description":"Icelandic politician, 2nd President of Iceland (1894-1972) Governor of Surtsey (1963-1968)","enwiki":"Ásgeir Ásgeirsson","summary":"Ásgeir Ásgeirsson (Icelandic: [ˈausceir̥ ˈausceir̥sɔn]; 13 May 1894 – 15 September 1972) was the second president of Iceland; he served from 1952 to 1968. He also served as the prime minister of Iceland from 3 June 1932 to 28 July 1934 for the Progressive Party. Ásgeir is the only person in Iceland to date to serve both as president and prime minister. He was a Freemason and served as grand master of the Icelandic Order of Freemasons.","image":"Iceland President Ásgeir Ásgeirsson. Jerusalem 1966 (D803-058) (cropped).jpg","birth":1894.3616438356164,"death":1972.704918032787},"Q24045706":{"label":"Guðni Jóhannesson","description":"President of Iceland (2016-2024)","enwiki":"Guðni Th. Jóhannesson","summary":"Guðni Thorlacius Jóhannesson (Icelandic pronunciation: [ˈkvʏðnɪ ˈtʰɔrlaˌsiʏs ˈjouːhanɛsɔn];\nborn 26 June 1968) is an Icelandic historian and politician who served as the sixth president of Iceland from 2016 to 2024.\nA career historian, Guðni was a professor at the University of Iceland before running for president in 2016. His field of research is modern Icelandic history, and he has published works on the Cod Wars, the 2008–2011 Icelandic financial crisis and the Icelandic presidency, among other topics. He also worked as a translator.","image":"Guðni Th. Jóhannesson at the Enthronement of Naruhito (1).jpg","birth":1968.483606557377,"death":null},"Q310396":{"label":"Kristján Eldjárn","description":"Icelandic politician, 3rd President of Iceland (1916-1982)","enwiki":"Kristján Eldjárn","summary":"Kristján Eldjárn (Icelandic: [ˈkʰrɪstjaun ˈɛltjaurtn̥]; 6 December 1916 – 14 September 1982) was the third president of Iceland, serving from 1968 to 1980.","image":"Kristján Eldjárn (1982).jpg","birth":1916.9289617486338,"death":1982.7013698630137},"Q57284":{"label":"Ólafur Ragnar Grímsson","description":"Icelandic politician, 5th President of Iceland","enwiki":"Ólafur Ragnar Grímsson","summary":"Ólafur Ragnar Grímsson (Icelandic: [ˈouːlavʏr ˈraknar ˈkrimsɔn] ; born 14 May 1943) is an Icelandic politician who was the fifth president of Iceland, serving from 1996 to 2016. He was previously a member of the Icelandic Parliament for the People's Alliance and served as Minister of Finance from 1988 to 1991.\nSince the end of his presidency, Ólafur has been serving as Chairman of the Arctic Circle, a non-profit organization, and as Chairman of the International Renewable Energy Agency's Global Commission on the Geopolitics of Energy Transformation.","image":"Olafur Ragnar Grimsson - World Economic Forum Annual Meeting Davos 2010.jpg","birth":1943.3643835616438,"death":null}},"media":{"Flag of Iceland (1918–1944).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Flag_of_Iceland_%281918%E2%80%931944%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_Iceland_%281918%E2%80%931944%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Iceland_(1918%E2%80%931944).svg","author":"Original: Matthías Þórðarson Vector:  Gustavo Ronconi","license":"Public domain"},"Kingdom of Iceland Coat of Arms.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Kingdom_of_Iceland_Coat_of_Arms.svg/330px-Kingdom_of_Iceland_Coat_of_Arms.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Iceland_Coat_of_Arms.svg","author":"Fenn-O-maniC","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},"Flag of Iceland.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Flag_of_Iceland.svg/330px-Flag_of_Iceland.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https:/